@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started
@cindex getting started

This chapter is a guided tour of @code{beads.el}.  It walks one
reproducible scenario through the deterministic demo project both this
manual and the @code{gascity.el} manual use, and then explains the
interaction patterns every view shares.  Create the demo project first
(@pxref{The Shared Demo Project}), install the package
(@pxref{Installation and Requirements}), and keep @ref{Concepts} handy
for the vocabulary; the commands behind the walkthrough are in
@ref{Issue Workflow}.

@menu
* The Shared Walkthrough::  Create, list, show, triage, unblock, close, maintain.
* Interface Concepts::      Modes, transients, actions, movement, stores.
@end menu

@node The Shared Walkthrough
@section The Shared Walkthrough
@cindex walkthrough
@cindex getting started walkthrough

This is the one walkthrough both manuals share.  It assumes the
canonical demo project exists, @command{bd} is on @env{PATH}, and
@code{beads.el} is loaded from @file{lisp/} (or installed).  Create
the project with @code{docs/manual/screenshots/fixture.sh create};
@xref{The Shared Demo Project}, for the exact @file{/tmp/demo-project}
path, the frozen dataset and its hash, the isolation guarantee, and
the cross-repo contract with @code{gascity.el}.  Start a
@w{@code{emacs -Q}} session, open @file{/tmp/demo-project} as the
current directory, and follow the steps in order.  Every step names the
exact keys to press, the buffer that opens, the echo-area or transient
feedback to expect, and the screenshot that shows the result.

The four shared demo steps, in the order both manuals describe them, are:
triage @code{demo-3}, unblock @code{demo-4}, close @code{demo-6}, and
inspect the @code{demo-10} epic.  The beads.el chapter expands them into
the full arc: create, list, show, claim and update, dependency, close,
and maintenance.

@menu
* Create an Issue::
* List the Issues::
* Show an Issue::
* Triage demo-3 and Update It::
* Unblock demo-4::
* Close and Reopen demo-6::
* Inspect the demo-10 Epic::
* Open the Dispatch and Maintenance Menus::
@end menu

@node Create an Issue
@subsection Create an Issue
@cindex create an issue
@cindex board

@deffn Command beads
Open the Beads board for the current project.  This is the primary entry
point of @code{beads.el}; it opens the full board (@code{beads-dashboard}).
Press @kbd{?} inside the buffer for the @code{beads-dispatch} menu.
@end deffn

Open the board with @kbd{M-x beads}.  The buffer is named
@code{*beads-dashboard<demo-project>*} and its header names the store
(@pxref{fig-dashboard-dark}).

Create a bead:

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{?} to open the dispatch menu.
@item
Press @kbd{c} to open the compose buffer
@code{*beads-create[demo-project]*} (@pxref{fig-compose-dark}).
@item
Type @kbd{Walkthrough: document the shared demo} on the first line.
@item
Press @kbd{RET} twice and type a description, for example
@kbd{Created from the shared demo walkthrough.}.
@item
Press @kbd{C-c C-c} to submit.  The echo area reports
@code{Created issue demo-<id>: Walkthrough: document the shared demo}
(the generated id keeps the @code{demo} prefix, for example
@code{demo-anv}) and open lists refresh.
@end enumerate

@kbd{C-c C-k} cancels the compose buffer instead.

@node List the Issues
@subsection List the Issues
@cindex listing issues

Press @kbd{?} and then @kbd{l} to list every bead, closed ones included.
The buffer is named @code{*beads-list[demo-project]*} and opens in
@code{beads-list-mode} (@pxref{fig-list-dark}).  The columns are ID, Type,
Status, Priority, Agents, and Title, grouped by status.  The demo state is
visible at a
glance: @code{demo-3} is @code{in_progress} in the warning face
(@code{beads-list-status-in-progress}), @code{demo-4} is
@code{blocked} in the error face (@code{beads-list-status-blocked}), and
@code{demo-6} is @code{closed} in the shadow face
(@code{beads-list-status-closed}).  The new bead from the previous step
appears in the same list.

Move with @kbd{TAB} and @kbd{S-TAB}; @kbd{SPC} previews the bead at point
in another window.

@node Show an Issue
@subsection Show an Issue
@cindex showing an issue

With point on @code{demo-3}, press @kbd{RET}.  The detail buffer is named
@code{*beads-show[demo-project]/demo-3 Fix crash when opening an e...*}: the
issue title follows the id and is truncated to 30 characters with an
ellipsis when longer.  It opens in @code{beads-show-mode}
(@pxref{fig-show-dark}).  The header renders the
stored status @code{in_progress} as @code{In Progress} in the warning
face, the priority 0 in the critical face, and the assignee
@code{demo}.  The body renders sections for the description,
dependencies, blocks, labels, and comments.

@kbd{TAB} and @kbd{S-TAB} move between headings and references, @kbd{SPC}
folds the section at point, @kbd{g} refreshes, and @kbd{q} quits.  Follow
a reference with @kbd{RET}.

@node Triage demo-3 and Update It
@subsection Triage demo-3 and Update It
@cindex triage
@cindex claim

With the @code{demo-3} detail buffer current, triage the bead in place:

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C} to claim it.  The echo area reports
@code{Claimed 1 issue(s)} (the immediate refresh then reports
@code{Refreshed demo-3}) and the assignee stays @code{demo} (the actor
the fixture uses).
@item
Press @kbd{#} to set priority.  At the @code{Priority for demo-3:}
prompt choose @code{1 - High}.  The echo area reports
@code{Set priority to 1 for 1 issue(s)} and the priority renders in the
@code{beads-list-priority-high} face (warning, bold); the refresh then
reports @code{Refreshed demo-3}.
@item
Press @kbd{s} to set status.  At the @code{Status for demo-3:} prompt
choose @code{in_progress}.  The echo area reports
@code{Set status to ‘in_progress’ for 1 issue(s)} (Emacs renders the
quotes around the value as curly quotes) and the status renders in the
warning face; the refresh then reports @code{Refreshed demo-3}.
@end enumerate

The fixture already starts @code{demo-3} in progress; pressing the keys
records your actor as owner and demonstrates the update path the rest of
the manual uses.  Refresh the list with @kbd{g} to see the new priority
and the changed @strong{Updated} time; the list has no owner column, so
the @strong{Agents} column stays empty and the assignee is shown in the
detail header instead.

@node Unblock demo-4
@subsection Unblock demo-4
@cindex unblock
@cindex dependency

@code{demo-4} is blocked by @code{demo-3}.  Resolve the blocker, then
reopen the dependent bead:

@enumerate
@item
With the @code{demo-3} detail buffer current, press @kbd{d}.  At the
@code{Close demo-3 reason:} prompt type @kbd{blocker resolved} and press
@kbd{RET}.  The echo area reports @code{Closed 1 issue(s)} (then
@code{Refreshed demo-3}).
@item
Open @code{demo-4} with @kbd{M-x beads-show} and choose @code{demo-4},
or follow the @strong{Blocks} reference from @code{demo-3}.  The detail
buffer renders @code{demo-4} as @code{Blocked} with a dependency on
@code{demo-3}.
@item
Press @kbd{s} and choose @code{open}.  The echo area reports
@code{Set status to ‘open’ for 1 issue(s)}.
@item
Press @kbd{?} and then @kbd{r} to open the ready list
@code{*beads-ready[demo-project]*}.  @code{demo-4} is now listed as ready
(@pxref{fig-ready-dark}), and the blocked list (@kbd{?} @kbd{b},
@pxref{fig-blocked-dark}) no longer shows it.
@end enumerate

Closing @code{demo-3} satisfies its dependency; setting @code{demo-4} to
@code{open} moves it into the ready frontier.  The two updates can be
done in either order.

@node Close and Reopen demo-6
@subsection Close and Reopen demo-6
@cindex close
@cindex reopen

@code{demo-6} starts closed.  Open the list with @kbd{?} @kbd{l}; the
closed section is on a later page, so press @kbd{]} until the
@code{demo-6} row is visible (or press @kbd{/} and filter by status
@code{closed}).  Then:

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{o} to reopen.  At the @code{Reopen demo-6 reason:} prompt
press @kbd{RET} to accept the empty reason.  The echo area reports
@code{Reopened 1 issue(s)} and the status changes to @code{open} in the
success face (the list refresh then reports @code{Refreshed 13 issues}).
The refresh regroups the rows by status, so put point back on the
@code{demo-6} row before the next step; a context action acts on the issue
at point and the row under point can change when the sections move.
@item
Press @kbd{d} to close it again.  At the @code{Close demo-6 reason:}
prompt type @kbd{walkthrough} and press @kbd{RET}.  The echo area
reports @code{Closed 1 issue(s)} and the status returns to
@code{closed} in the shadow face.
@end enumerate

In the list (@kbd{?} @kbd{l}) the @code{demo-6} row is now grey
(@pxref{fig-list-dark}); the same keys work on marked rows and on
every row of a list buffer.

@node Inspect the demo-10 Epic
@subsection Inspect the demo-10 Epic
@cindex epic
@cindex parent-child

Run @kbd{M-x beads-epic-menu}.  The transient offers @kbd{s} (status
overview) and @kbd{c} (close eligible); press @kbd{s}.

The buffer @code{*beads-epic-status[demo-project]*} lists
@code{demo-10}, @strong{Epic: rewrite the user manual}, with
@code{Progress: 0/2 children closed (0%)} in the low-progress face.
Press @kbd{SPC} on the epic to expand its children, @code{demo-11} and
@code{demo-12}; press @kbd{RET} to open the epic's detail buffer, which
lists the same two children under its @samp{BLOCKS} section (they are the
epic's @code{parent-of} edges) as shown in @ref{fig-epic-dark}.
@kbd{g} refreshes and @kbd{q} quits.

Because both children are open, @code{demo-10} is not eligible for
closure; that is what @kbd{c} would close once every child is done.

@node Open the Dispatch and Maintenance Menus
@subsection Open the Dispatch and Maintenance Menus
@cindex dispatch menu
@cindex maintenance menu

The walkthrough ends at the two menus every other chapter uses.  From any
beads buffer, press @kbd{?} to open the dispatch menu: @strong{Issues},
@strong{Workflow}, @strong{Views}, and @strong{Manage}, with a
@strong{Maintenance} row at the foot (@pxref{fig-dispatch-dark}).  The
keys used above live there: @kbd{c} create, @kbd{l} list, @kbd{r} ready,
@kbd{b} blocked, @kbd{d} dependencies, @kbd{C} claim, @kbd{s} status,
and @kbd{#} priority.

Press @kbd{!} from the dispatch menu (or @kbd{M-x beads-maintenance}) to
open the maintenance menu (@pxref{fig-maintenance-dark}).  Its
@strong{Issue Lifecycle}, @strong{Views & Reports}, @strong{Issue
Details}, @strong{Events}, and @strong{Workflow} groups are the daily
tools; @kbd{C-x l} cycles the transient level to reveal the
@strong{Database}, @strong{Structure}, and @strong{Data & Sync} groups.
Press @kbd{q} to quit a transient.

That is the whole arc: create, list, show, claim and update, dependency,
close, and maintenance.  The task chapters expand each part:
@ref{Issue Workflow} for the commands, @ref{Dashboards and Views} for the
read-only surfaces, @ref{Formulas Molecules and Epics} for epics and
molecules, and @ref{Maintenance and Extending} for the admin menus.

@node Interface Concepts
@section Interface Concepts
@cindex interface concepts
@cindex interaction patterns

@code{beads.el} uses three interaction patterns, modeled on Magit and
Forge.  Understanding them tells you where a command lives and what a key
will do.

@menu
* Modes and Buffers::
* Transients::              The dispatch and maintenance menus.
* Context-Aware Actions::
* Compose Buffers::
* Marks and Bulk Operations::
* One Movement Scheme::
* Store Scoping::
@end menu

@node Modes and Buffers
@subsection Modes and Buffers
@cindex modes
@cindex buffers

The interface is built from a small set of modes.  The porcelain board
uses @code{vui} for heterogeneous, collapsible sections, and
@code{tabulated-list-mode} for homogeneous tables.

@deffn {Major Mode} beads-dashboard-mode
The full project board, opened by @kbd{M-x beads}.  Each section loads
asynchronously and folds independently.
@end deffn

@deffn {Major Mode} beads-list-mode
A @code{tabulated-list-mode} derivative for browsing issues.  It supports
sorting, filtering, marking, and bulk operations.
@end deffn

@deffn {Major Mode} beads-show-mode
A read-only section view for one issue, with section navigation,
reference following, and inline editing.
@end deffn

Buffer names carry the project so several stores can be open at once,
and are qualified by branch or remote when needed (see
@file{beads-buffer.el}):

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{*beads-dashboard<PROJECT>*}
@item
@code{*beads-list[PROJECT]*}, @code{*beads-ready[PROJECT]*},
@code{*beads-blocked[PROJECT]*}
@item
@code{*beads-show[PROJECT]/ISSUE-ID TITLE*} (the title is truncated to 30
characters with @samp{...} and omitted only when the issue is opened
without one)
@item
@code{*beads-agent[PROJECT]/TYPE#N*}
@end itemize

The board is the one view that brackets the project with angle brackets
instead of square brackets; a remote board is qualified as
@code{*beads-dashboard<REMOTE|PROJECT>*}.  The other views use square
brackets, and the project is qualified by its branch when the store is
scoped to one.

@node Transients
@subsection Transients: Dispatch and Maintenance
@cindex transients
@cindex dispatch menu
@cindex maintenance menu

@code{beads.el} keeps Magit's status/dispatch split:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Board} (@kbd{M-x beads}) --- the front door is the board, not a
transient.  @kbd{M-x beads} runs @code{beads-dashboard}.
@item
@strong{Dispatch menu} (@kbd{?}) --- @code{beads-dispatch} is the command
menu, opened with @kbd{?} from any beads buffer (or @kbd{M-x
beads-dispatch}).  It groups the common flows into @strong{Issues},
@strong{Workflow}, @strong{Views}, and @strong{Manage}, with a
@strong{Maintenance} row at the foot.  Downstream packages append their
own groups through @code{beads-menu-providers}.
@item
@strong{Maintenance menu} (@kbd{?} @kbd{!}, or @kbd{M-x
beads-maintenance}) --- @code{beads-maintenance} is the single admin and
infrastructure menu.  It is the successor of the former
@code{beads-ops-menu} and @code{beads-advanced-menu}, which no longer
exist.
@end enumerate

@deffn Command beads-dashboard
Open the full project board.  The board resolves the Beads store from
@code{default-directory}, unless @code{:directory} is given
(@pxref{Store Scoping}).
@end deffn

@anchor{fig-dashboard-dark}
@shot{dashboard-dark, The project board.}

@deffn Command beads-dispatch
Open the dispatch menu: the common issue and workflow commands grouped
into @strong{Issues}, @strong{Workflow}, @strong{Views}, and
@strong{Manage}, with a @strong{Maintenance} row underneath.
@end deffn

@deffn Command beads-maintenance
Open the maintenance menu: admin and infrastructure commands such as
Dolt and repository maintenance, terminal backends, configuration, and
the audit.
@end deffn

The menus are ordinary transient prefixes defined with
@code{beads-define-prefix}.  Every suffix runs in the directory the menu
was opened for, so a menu opened from another project acts on that
project's store.  The bulk of the per-command menus is generated from
command-class slot metadata; @pxref{Issue Workflow}.

@node Context-Aware Actions
@subsection Context-Aware Actions
@cindex context actions

@emph{Pattern 1} commands are single-key actions bound directly in mode
maps.  They detect the issue at point (falling back to
@code{completing-read}) and act immediately.

Examples in @code{beads-list-mode} and @code{beads-show-mode}:

@table @kbd
@kindex C
@item C
Claim the issue at point.
@kindex d
@item d
Close the issue at point (prompts for a reason).
@kindex o
@item o
Reopen a closed issue.  This is list-only; in @code{beads-show-mode}
@kbd{o} follows the reference at point in another window, and reopen is
in the show actions transient.
@kindex s
@item s
Set status.
@kindex #
@item #
Set priority.
@kindex E
@item E
Edit the description.  This is show-only; in a list, press @kbd{RET} to
open the detail view first.
@kindex N
@item N
Add a comment.  This is show-only; @kbd{N} in a list moves to the next
section.
@end table

@node Compose Buffers
@subsection Compose Buffers
@cindex compose buffers

@emph{Pattern 3} commands open a compose buffer for content creation or
editing.  The buffer uses @code{gfm-mode} (or @code{text-mode} as a
fallback) and follows the @code{with-editor} convention: the first line
is the title, everything after the first blank line is the Markdown
description.

@table @kbd
@kindex C-c C-c
@item C-c C-c
Submit (create the issue, save the edit, post the comment).
@kindex C-c C-k
@item C-c C-k
Cancel and restore the previous window configuration.
@kindex C-c C-a
@item C-c C-a
Open the metadata sidebar (type, priority, labels, parent).
@end table

@node Marks and Bulk Operations
@subsection Marks and Bulk Operations
@cindex marks
@cindex bulk operations

In @code{beads-list-mode} you can mark several issues and act on them as
a batch:

@table @kbd
@kindex m
@item m
Mark the issue at point.
@kindex u
@item u
Unmark the issue at point.
@kindex U
@item U
Unmark all issues.
@kindex *
@kindex !
@item @kbd{*} @kbd{!}
Mark all issues.
@end table

When marks are active, context-aware actions such as @kbd{d} and @kbd{o}
operate on every marked issue.  Bulk-specific operations live under the
@kbd{B} prefix: @kbd{B s} status, @kbd{B p} priority, @kbd{B c} close,
@kbd{B o} reopen.

@node One Movement Scheme
@subsection One Movement Scheme
@cindex movement
@cindex thing

Every view moves with one primitive, @code{beads-thing}: @kbd{TAB} and
@kbd{S-TAB} move between things and wrap, and @kbd{SPC} toggles the thing
at point (folding a section, previewing an issue, and so on).  In
tabulated lists each row is a thing without visible stamping.
@xref{Issue Workflow}, for how movement applies to lists and show
buffers.  The movement commands are @code{beads-thing-forward}
(@kbd{TAB}), @code{beads-thing-backward} (@kbd{S-TAB}), and
@code{beads-thing-toggle} (@kbd{SPC}).

@fnindex beads-thing-forward
@fnindex beads-thing-backward
@fnindex beads-thing-toggle

@node Store Scoping
@subsection Store Scoping
@anchor{store-scoping}
@cindex store scoping
@cindex store directory

The high-level entry points @code{beads-show}, @code{beads-ready},
@code{beads-blocked}, @code{beads-list-issues} and @code{beads-dashboard}
accept an optional @code{:directory} keyword.  When non-nil, the
operation is scoped to the Beads store at that directory instead of the
one resolved from @code{default-directory}.  This matters when a shared
Dolt server could otherwise route the working directory to another
project's database.

@lisp
(beads-show "bd-42" :directory "/path/to/project")
(beads-dashboard :directory "/path/to/project")
@end lisp

@defvr {Buffer-Local Variable} beads-store-directory
The Beads store a buffer acts on.  The directory passed to a high-level
entry point becomes the buffer's store: every @command{bd} command run
from that buffer whose @code{directory} slot is unset gets
@command{--directory}, so refreshes and actions keep acting on that
store, and buffers opened from it inherit it.
@end defvr

